Process priority and resource control — CPU scheduling, I/O priority, and CPU usage limiting.

@text
nice — Set CPU Priority at Launch

Nice values range from -20 (highest priority) to 19 (lowest). Default is 0. Only root can set negative values.

@code bash
# Run a command with lower priority (nicer to other processes)
nice -n 10 make -j$(nproc)

# Run with lowest priority
nice -n 19 find / -name "*.log" -size +100M

# Run with higher priority (requires root)
sudo nice -n -10 /usr/bin/important-task

# Run with highest priority (requires root)
sudo nice -n -20 /usr/bin/critical-task

# Default nice value when using nice without -n
nice make        # runs at nice 10

@text
renice — Change Priority of Running Process

@code bash
# Reduce priority of a running process
renice 10 -p 1234

# Set highest nice (lowest priority)
renice 19 -p 1234

# Increase priority (requires root)
sudo renice -5 -p 1234

# Renice all processes by a user
sudo renice 10 -u username

# Renice a process group
sudo renice 10 -g 1234

# Check current nice value
ps -o pid,ni,comm -p 1234

@text
ionice — I/O Scheduling Priority

Controls disk I/O priority. Three scheduling classes: Realtime (1), Best-effort (2), Idle (3).

@code bash
# Run a command with idle I/O priority (won't interfere with other I/O)
ionice -c 3 rsync -a /source/ /dest/

# Run with best-effort, low priority (class 2, level 7)
ionice -c 2 -n 7 tar czf backup.tar.gz /data

# Run with realtime I/O (requires root, use carefully)
sudo ionice -c 1 -n 0 dd if=/dev/sda of=/dev/sdb bs=4M

# Check I/O priority of a process
ionice -p 1234

# Change I/O priority of a running process
ionice -c 3 -p 1234
sudo ionice -c 2 -n 0 -p 1234

# I/O scheduling classes:
# 1 = Realtime    — highest priority, preempts others (root only)
#                   Levels 0-7, lower = higher priority
# 2 = Best-effort — default class
#                   Levels 0-7, lower = higher priority
# 3 = Idle        — only gets I/O when nobody else needs it
#                   No priority levels

# Combine nice and ionice for background tasks
nice -n 19 ionice -c 3 updatedb

@text
chrt — Realtime Scheduling

Set the realtime scheduling policy and priority. Use with extreme caution — can starve other processes.

@code bash
# Run with SCHED_FIFO (realtime first-in-first-out)
sudo chrt -f 50 /usr/bin/realtime-app

# Run with SCHED_RR (realtime round-robin)
sudo chrt -r 50 /usr/bin/realtime-app

# Run with SCHED_BATCH (throughput-oriented)
chrt -b 0 make -j$(nproc)

# Run with SCHED_IDLE (very low priority)
chrt -i 0 find / -name "*.tmp" -delete

# Run with SCHED_DEADLINE (requires root, kernel support)
sudo chrt -d --sched-runtime 500000 --sched-deadline 1000000 \
  --sched-period 1000000 0 /usr/bin/deadline-app

# Check scheduling policy of a running process
chrt -p 1234

# Change policy of a running process
sudo chrt -f -p 50 1234

# View valid priority ranges
chrt --max

# Scheduling policies:
# SCHED_OTHER (0)  — default time-sharing (use nice for priority)
# SCHED_FIFO  (1)  — realtime FIFO, runs until it yields or higher priority preempts
# SCHED_RR    (2)  — realtime round-robin, like FIFO with time slice
# SCHED_BATCH (3)  — batch scheduling, for CPU-intensive non-interactive tasks
# SCHED_IDLE  (5)  — extremely low priority
# Priority range for realtime: 1-99 (higher = more priority)

@text
cpulimit — Limit CPU Usage Percentage

@code bash
# Install cpulimit
sudo pacman -S cpulimit

# Limit a running process to 50% CPU
cpulimit -p 1234 -l 50

# Limit by process name
cpulimit -e ffmpeg -l 200    # 200% = 2 full cores

# Launch a command with CPU limit
cpulimit -l 75 -- make -j$(nproc)

# Run in background
cpulimit -p 1234 -l 50 -b

# Limit values: per-core percentage
# 50  = 50% of one core
# 100 = one full core
# 200 = two full cores

@text
cgroups — Resource Groups

@code bash
# Check cgroup version
mount | grep cgroup
stat -f --format=%T /sys/fs/cgroup/    # cgroup2fs = v2, tmpfs = v1

# Limit CPU for a group of processes (cgroup v2)
sudo mkdir /sys/fs/cgroup/limited
echo "50000 100000" | sudo tee /sys/fs/cgroup/limited/cpu.max  # 50% of one CPU
echo $$ | sudo tee /sys/fs/cgroup/limited/cgroup.procs         # add current shell

# Limit memory
echo "512M" | sudo tee /sys/fs/cgroup/limited/memory.max

# View current cgroup of a process
cat /proc/1234/cgroup
